1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Checkpoints nearly doubled timely uploads in our Flagsmith test. We kept them off by
default.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Here's what changed that decision.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Two clients send the same file. At seventy percent, both connections drop at exactly
the same byte.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:19,000
The receiver kept the confirmed chunks. Only the resumable upload finished on time.

5
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Now try a stable link.

6
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Each request takes two hundred milliseconds to acknowledge. Eight checkpoint
requests consume the entire deadline.

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:33,000
This time only the whole file passed. Every success requires a matching server
digest.

8
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:40,000
For the recorded experiment, Flagsmith assigned each of one hundred sixty simulated
visitors to one strategy.

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:47,000
Thirty-eight of eighty-three control uploads and sixty-eight of seventy-seven
resumable uploads finished on time.

10
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:54,000
But on the high-latency link, whole-file retry won eleven of eleven. Resume won zero
of nine.

11
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:59,000
We retained control. Let's check the released flag with a fresh upload.

12
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:03,000
The official SDK reads the current flag from Flagsmith.

13
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Control selected whole-file retry. This fresh server receipt verifies that choice
without adding a visitor to the experiment.
